# The container entrypoint is executed by the kernel, which reads the shebang
# byte for byte. A checkout on a machine with core.autocrlf=true would give it
# CRLF endings, and the resulting image fails to start with a message that names
# the interpreter rather than the script:
#
#   exec ./docker-entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# because /bin/sh\r does not exist. Pinning LF keeps an image built on any
# platform runnable on the Linux host it is deployed to.
*.sh text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf

# Everything else that is read inside the container. Python tolerates CRLF, but
# there is no reason for it to differ between a Windows and a Linux checkout.
*.py text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.ini text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.lock text eol=lf
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
